{
  "reasoning": "The lecture explains how the Unity developer environment supports implementation of an XR system. I kept the opening, objective, and high-level overview of Unity's graphics-pipeline support together as the first sub-goal, then cut at sentence 16, where the lecturer moves to render-pipeline implementation options. I cut at sentence 33 because it bridges into the built-in pipeline features and makes 'this pipeline' self-contained for the object-definition segment. I then separated object definition, scene creation, lighting/effects, view transformation, library support, physics, animation, and audio because each gives the learner a distinct Unity capability, and I made the closing summary a final segment.",
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 1,
      "subgoal": "Explain how the Unity developer environment supports graphics-pipeline operations, including the stages it covers and customization options."
    },
    {
      "start": 16,
      "subgoal": "Compare Unity's built-in, Universal, High Definition, and custom scriptable render pipelines."
    },
    {
      "start": 33,
      "subgoal": "Describe how Unity's built-in render pipeline supports object definition using model files, meshes, materials, and textures."
    },
    {
      "start": 66,
      "subgoal": "Explain how Unity supports scene and environment creation using terrain tools, tree/SpeedTree support, and skyboxes."
    },
    {
      "start": 80,
      "subgoal": "Describe Unity's lighting support and visual effects such as halo, lens flare, and line renderer."
    },
    {
      "start": 92,
      "subgoal": "Explain how Unity's camera component supports the view-transformation stage of the graphics pipeline."
    },
    {
      "start": 99,
      "subgoal": "Describe how Unity implements the graphics pipeline through native graphics libraries and AutoGraphics API selection."
    },
    {
      "start": 131,
      "subgoal": "Describe how Unity's physics engine simulates realistic motion using rigid bodies, colliders, joints, and articulation."
    },
    {
      "start": 177,
      "subgoal": "Explain how Unity's animation system supports importing, retargeting, and managing animation clips."
    },
    {
      "start": 185,
      "subgoal": "Explain how Unity's audio source, audio listener, and audio filter components support immersive audio."
    },
    {
      "start": 203,
      "subgoal": "Summarize the main points of the lecture and preview the next part on scripting and SDK support."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "reasoning": "I merged old Segment 2 into the first segment because Unity's render-pipeline options are a direct continuation of the opening objective and the overview of Unity's graphics-pipeline support, making the first segment more complete. I merged old Segments 4, 5, and 6 because scene creation, lighting/visual effects, and view transformation are short adjacent built-in-pipeline stages that together form one coherent sub-goal about Unity's visual-stage support. I merged old Segment 9 with the following audio segment because animation and audio support are short adjacent features that together form a more meaningful sub-goal about immersive non-graphics support. The remaining segments were kept because they already form whole, self-contained steps.",
